WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
How can the same white coat protect a hare and put it in danger?

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.880
Watch this Montana hillside change around one hare.

3
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:16.213
Begin in fall. The ground is brown, and the hare is brown too.

4
00:00:17.573 --> 00:00:19.993
Its outline blends into leaves and tree shadows.

5
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:23.087
Now snow covers the hillside.

6
00:00:24.327 --> 00:00:27.287
The hare's large back feet spread its weight on soft snow.

7
00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:30.647
Fur underneath helps keep those feet warm.

8
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.010
The hare also grows white winter fur.

9
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.750
This coloring is camouflage because it helps the hare blend into its background.

10
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.010
Camouflage never makes it invisible.

11
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:46.963
Spring comes early, but the hare is still white.

12
00:00:48.263 --> 00:00:52.183
When the snow disappears, will the hare become harder or easier to spot?

13
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:01.347
The bare ground returns, and the white hare stands out.

14
00:01:02.587 --> 00:01:04.527
The predator can notice it more easily.

15
00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:08.227
The helpful winter coat now carries a cost.

16
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.960
An adaptation is an inherited trait that helps survival and reproduction in a

17
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.000
particular ecosystem.

18
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.780
The coat and feet are physical adaptations.

19
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:25.143
A behavioral adaptation is an action or pattern of actions.

20
00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:30.863
Migration is a regular journey between places used in different seasons.

21
00:01:32.243 --> 00:01:35.763
The animal does something, rather than having another body part.

22
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.367
Physical adaptations do different jobs.

23
00:01:40.727 --> 00:01:43.807
A heron's long legs reach into deeper water for fish.

24
00:01:45.027 --> 00:01:47.967
A turtle's shell shields its soft body from predators.

25
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.720
Hibernation crosses the simple groups.

26
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.940
A groundhog becomes deeply inactive, its heart slows, and its body cools.

27
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.520
That is behavior plus a change inside the body.

28
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:06.603
A trade-off means a trait helps in one way but costs in another.

29
00:02:07.903 --> 00:02:10.503
The white coat works well only when the background fits.

30
00:02:11.617 --> 00:02:14.437
The turtle's protective shell is heavy to carry.

31
00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:17.637
No living thing is perfectly adapted.

32
00:02:19.037 --> 00:02:22.077
Conditions change, and a useful feature can bring a cost.

33
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:29.250
Adaptations help in particular settings; populations change over generations, not

34
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:35.170
individuals. One hare cannot choose a new inherited coat because the snow vanished.

35
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:38.893
Hares in one forest are not identical.

36
00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:42.593
Some begin changing color earlier than others.

37
00:02:43.953 --> 00:02:48.673
These differences among individuals are variation, and they may matter when seasons

38
00:02:48.673 --> 00:02:49.673
shift.

39
00:02:50.217 --> 00:02:52.877
Variation also appears across places.

40
00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:58.237
In some places with little snow, snowshoe hares stay brown all winter.

41
00:02:59.477 --> 00:03:01.857
The useful coat still depends on the setting.

42
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.460
So the same coat can hide or expose a hare.

43
00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.860
Next, the field evidence asks whether that mismatch changes survival across the

44
00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:17.240
population. We will also see what the evidence cannot tell us about any one death.
